@article{Smith1994,
abstract = {Chapter 1: Vico versus Descartes Hermans and Kempen open their chapter with an observation from Levin (1988): "...the Cartesian self came out as a 'self of reason completely purged of body and feeling, a self without shadows, a self totally transparent to itself, totally knowing of itself, totally self-possessed, totally certain of itself.'" [p. 1] They contrast Descartes' vision with that of the founder of the philosophy of history, Giambattista Vico (born in 1668). Vico "...rejected Descartes' method of systematic doubt since it ignored the role of historical knowledge in human awareness. Vico argued that Cogito as the final certainty is a serious reduction of the human condition, resulting in an historical and disembodied conception of the mind. Instead, Vico elaborates on the view that mind and body are inseparable..." [p. 1] The most important opposition in the works of Descartes is that between mind and body. "Closely related to the mind-body dualism is the separation of self and other". [p. 2] According to Hermans and Kempen both of the fundamental distinctions that Descartes made (an opposition of mind and body and an opposition of self and other) are both unrealistic: they do not stroke with our everyday experience. Vico's position on this issue was exactly the opposite of Descartes. He believed that human knowledge was embodied, and that "...knowing is relating to other people." [p. 4] Hermans and Kempen state that Vico could justly be viewed as the father of social constructivism. [p. 5] He put a lot of emphasis on the notion of imagination. This concept will also play a large role in this book by Hermans and Kempen. Chapter 2: The narrative construction of reality In this chapter Hermans and Kempen discuss the idea of a root metaphor, first introduced by Pepper (1942) and later on applied to psychology by Sarbin (1986). Pepper states that "...the hypothesis that people develop about their world are derived from a basic root metaphor guiding their perceptions and thinking. A root metaphor not only offers a basic perspective for perceiving, classifying, and interpreting events, but also constrains these activities. Metaphors, like spotlights, produce a particular view of reality. At the same time, however, each light is bound to a particular angle and, therefore, offers only one view. Metaphors create views and at the same time restrict these views." [p. 11] He goes on to make a distinction between four types of metaphors relevant for science: (1) Formism: "...stresses the organization of the environment on the basis of the form of things (big, small, short, long, round, angular)." [p. 11] It plays a role in distinguishing differences between people and objects in the world. (2) Mechanisism: "...considers the human organism as reactive from a basic state of rest and activity as a result of external stimulation." [p. 12] (3) Organicism: "considers the human organism as living and active. The components of the organism are seen as parts of and organized whole, and change is viewed not only quantitatively but also qualitatively (e.g., the growth and blossom of a plant). [...] There is a telos, a goal, that gives unity and direction to the organized process." [p. 13] As a comment on this perspective Hermans and Kempen write: "In more theoretical terms, organismic theories, in assuming a fixed sequence of developmental stages for all people irrespective of their sex, race, socioeconomic status, and family background, are quite insensitive to apparent differences among people and, in particular, do not take into account their individual histories." [p. 14] (4) Contextualism: This is the root metaphor that will guide Hermans and Kempen in this book. According to this metaphor people cannot be viewed separately from the contexts they live, work and develop themselves in. Hermans and Kempen show how narrative is a crucial element of human life: human beings are constantly ordering, revising and editing their life stories. They refer to Cohler (1982, 1988), who states that "...personal narratives represent internally consistent interpretations of the past as presently understood, the experienced present, and the anticipated future. Such a conception implies that a life story is never fixed." [p. 15] According to Bruner (1986) there are two "modes of thought" [p. 17]: (1) Storytelling (narrative thinking): stories are intended to convince the audience of the lifelikeness; (2) Argumentation (propositional thinking): argumentation is intended to convince the audience of their truth. [p. 17] Hermans and Kempen show that both emotions and actions are guided by narrative plots. They write: "Narratives provide the characters, ideas, settings, instruments, and procedures that individuals or groups may take as models for shaping their own behavior." [p. 22] An important aspect of narratives is the idea of emplotment: "...constructing and interconnecting events in such a way that meaningful structures are developed. Emplotment marks the difference between a chronicle and narrative construction." [p. 22] When we tell a story in chronological order it ends up being a long sequence of sentences starting with the words "and then". By using emplotment we creatively combine and recombine parts of the story and affect the order of the different parts of it. Through this we create a plot. When we tell stories to an audience it is not just the narrator who plays a role in the emplotment of those stories. The narratees (the people we tell the story to) are important in the process, too. Based on the amount of (assumed) common knowledge we omit parts of the story or add parts to it. The narratee helps us decide in which order to tell things and which parts to select. Two other concepts relevant in the theory of narratives are: (1) The dialectic relationship between the events of the story and its plot: "...the meaning of a particular event is produced by the recognition of how the event and plot interact, each providing form to the other. Events do not dictate any plot, and not every plot can order a set of events. An appropriate configuration emerges only after a moving back and forth between plot and events." [p. 25] (2) Themes: a "...theme allows the grasping together of the events as interrelated parts of the story. The theme functions as a guide for the selection of certain events as relevant and other events as irrelevant." [p. 25] Chapter 3: The decentralization of the self In the first part of this chapter Hermans and Kempen give an overview of the works of different thinkers in the tradition of pragmatism. They focus mainly on the works of Dewey, and in particular on his revision of the notion of the "subject". According to Dewey the subject cannot be viewed separately from the environment in which it operates. There is a dialectic relationship between the subject and its environment: on the one had the subject can act within and on the environment, thereby influencing the environment. On the other hand (and at the same time) the environment acts on the subject and influences the subject in its actions. This leads to a decentering of the subject: "...the subject is living between two centers: the subject is acting upon the world and the world is acting upon the subject. The interaction is so complex that the environment is in a way in the subject." In the second part of this chapter Hermans and Kempen contrast the pragmatic view with that of the French structuralists. There are a lot of similarities between the pragmatist perspective and that of the French structuralists. Both believe in the crucial importance of language as a mechanism of communicating symbols. Both emphasize the role language plays in human lives, both for communicating with others and for communicating with the self. Concerning the French structuralists Hermans and Kempen write: "...conscious subjectivity [...] is not seen as given at the outset of human condition, but as constituted only in an through language. The origin of subjectivity is not to be traced in the workings of the individual mind, but in the pervasive influence of symbolic systems. In this view the subject is deprived from its status as source and master of meaning. [...] In the structuralist view, the identity of the subject is essentially seen as a series of positions evoked as a response to the discourse of others. An important implication of this view is that I am, in principle, not able to find out who I am, because who I am has been an continues to be determined by how I am addressed by others. both by other human beings and my own immanent other..." [p. 31] Like for example George Herbert Mead the French structuralists also believe that there is no such things as the "ultimate meaning" of words etc. - words, and symbols in general, always derive their meaning from the contexts in which they are used by people - they derive their meaning from the discourses of certain cultures, places and times, and these discourses always keep on changing. There are differences between the structuralists and the pragmatists, too, though. Although both believe in the importance of language in the formation and sustenance of identity, the structuralists take a much more impersonal stance. They focus on the structures underlying the use of symbols (e.g. by focussing on signifiers and things signified). The pragmatists on the other hand focus primarily on the interpersonal aspect of identity. Whereas the structuralists view the human agency as a mainly passive receptor of a certain (ever-changing) identity, the pragmatists hold a more balanced view, wherein the agent is both active and passive. In the third part of the chapter Hermans and Kempen focus on the way the self is perceived in current research. There are a number of important issues: (1) Some researchers focus on the multifacetedness of self. They emphasize that the subject has a whole range of aspects, characteristics, r},
