@book{Morelli2025,
abstract = {Technology created modernity and modernity created technology. This means that a full comprehension of technology and modernity requires close examination of both. The problem is that there is a gap between technological studies and modernity theories, the first being mostly concerned with the micro and the empirical, the last being concerned with generalization on the macro level. This article is an attmept to offer a synthetic approach combining the two. Modernity theory: understanding the modern condition It is possible to make a distinction between cultural and epistemological theories of modernity (mostly found in humanities), and institutional ones (common in social theories). Cultural and Epistemological theories of modenrity: These theories usually focus on the ditinction between pre modern and modern modes of knowledge. They usually locate this shift in the 15th or 16th century Europe with the emergence of such concepts as the autonomous subject, mechanistic world picture, humanistic values and objective sientific inquiry. Some theories tie modernity with modernism, which was in fact a a much easier-to-define cultural movement which started at the mid 19th century and represented a revolt against traditonal concepts and values. The emergenc eof thsi movement was often explained by the social chaneges of the 19th century, including the space-time compression process. Modernism was also taken to stand, more generally, for the culture and ideology of modernity at large. In this sense, modernsim means positivism, rationalism, the belief in linear progress, standartization of knowledge, the belief in universal truth and the rational planning of social orders. Instituional theories of modernity, focus on the transition of social an institutional strucures, and locate the transition mostly in the 18th century, with the rise of industrial society. This tradition is as old as social theory itself. Modernity according to this view is a mode of social life or organization rather than a cultural or epistemological condition. The Modernization theories of the 50's and 60's were usually concerned with this view of modernity and attempted to explain its coming into being. [Critical theory tended to blend both traditions.] These theories usually do the following: 1. They draw a sharp distinction between modern and pre-modern. 2. They focus on the macro level 3. They describe historical processes that lead to modernity and the phases within it. 4. some theories offer a critique of the moden conditon. Some studies in social theory focus on specific phases within the modenr era they attempt to characterise. Others try to limit themselves to dealing with specific trnasitions which shaped different faces of the modern world. These can only be considered related to theories of modernity as long as they specifcally relate to it. Modernity and social theory The fathers of these theories, Marx and Weber, explain moderniry as resulting from differnet processes. Marx has pointed to the growing prodctivity of the late feudal economy that has enabled the transition to the capitalist phase, which was marked by different relations of production. Weber asserted that modernity was characterized by rationalization which originated from the Protestant working ethics that demaned hard work and the accumulation of wealth. Rational modenity according to Webeer has brought freedom, but also a new kind of bureaucratic oppression. Scholars if critical theories of modernity, especially from the Frankfurt school, combined elements from both Marx and Weber in their critique. Anthony gidens has more recently argued that modernity rests o 4 major institutions: industrialism, capitalism, surveillance and military power. One of the main reasons for the exceptional dynamism these modern insitutions exhibit is the seperation of time and space. through new time and spae organizing devices, from each other and from the contextual realities to which they were tied. Time and space become empty parameters that can be used for the construction of large scale systems. Late additions to modernity theories claim that towards the end of the 20th century we have entered a new phase in modernity or in other analyses, post modernity. Technology StudiesThis multi-disciplinary field emerged in the 1970's and the author wishes to focus on two streams within that field: 1. social studies of technology 2. history of technology Technological studies often focus on specific technologies and their relations to society, rather than generalize about "Technology" with a capital T. Some studies focus on the development of the technology, others on the impact it had on society and yet others on both deimentions. These would very often even deny a clear difference between society and technology and speak in terms of sociotechnologies (Bijker and Law 1992; Latour 1987; Callon 1987) [The geneology of social studies of technology) In contemporary SST "technology is regarded, in part or wholly, as a social construction that must be explaiend by reference to social processes, and within which no apeal can be made to objective standards of truth, efficiency and rationality. The history of technology is a much older field but in both fields most studies are in fact case studies. Theoretical claims of technology studies1. Technology is socially shaped and its developmet is not linear and does not emenates from some inner logic, but rather from its interaction with society. Most theorists agree that there is flexibility in the ways technology would be interpreted and used and that these implications are notpre-given or inscribed in the "body" of the technology. Usually it takes some time for a technology to stabalize, i.e. to attain stable relations and meanings with regards to specific societies and then, for at least some time, it becomes transparent. 2. Society is technologically shaped. This means not only that technology changes society or that it has side effects (like air pollution). It becomes part and parcell of the social fabric, a sociotechnical network. 3. According to some, it is actually impossibel to seperate human from non-human actors in these sociotechnical networks, and the technical facets to are attributed with agency. Technology is usually neglected in social theories of modernity, which tend to analyze modernity in institurional, cultural or epistemological terms. Such terms push technology to the background and present it, if at all, as a mere device by which the "real" agent realize modernity. Social theory is often neglected or objected in technological studies due to its tnedency towards generalizations, determinsim. The author at last turns to offer ways to close the gap between the different levels of analysis and the different units of analysis, so as to facilitate the interaction of modernity theory and technology studies.},
annote = {[relink-2026-07-26: 1 file path(s) updated for 07_THEORIE folder reorg, method=basename-match, tiers=['unique']]},
author = {Morelli, Michael},
booktitle = {Theology, Ethics, and Technology in the Work of Jacques Ellul and Paul Virilio},
doi = {10.5040/9781978733077.ch-1},
file = {:C\:/07_THEORIE/_Z-Library/Modernity and Technology by Thomas J. Misa, Philip Brey, Andrew Feenberg (z-lib.org).pdf:pdf},
isbn = {0262134217},
pages = {5--30},
title = {{War, Modernity, and Technology}},
year = {2025}
}
