@article{Bateson1972,
abstract = {The task of the scientist can be explained by dividing the elements of science into three parts: \n1/data (photographs, words, behaviours and any other already processed, displayed but not infered material which is in this sense raw and yet constructed) \n2/explanatory notions ("mind", "jealousy") and other heuristic concepts \n3/fundamentals of which there are few and all produced in the hard sciences (mathematical and physical laws) which are more or less universally true. \n"raw" data heuristic concepts fundamentals \n-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n----------------------------------> induction \ndeduction <------------------------------- \nMost humanities use induction and thus hope that they will at some point arrive at the fundamentals but this has not happend in a long tie and Bateson proposes tthat on needs to look to fundamentals and deduct heuristic concepts. He questions the general belief that the the hard sciences have utilized induction only. Bateson wants to link existing fundamentals with data. Freud thried so with the idea of energy but Bateson thinks this is flawed beause energy, in the end, is not but Mass?Velocity2 and neither mass nor velocity assists us in understanding human behaviour. \nContrarily, we need to look at the fundamentals of form because this is the stuff mental processes, ideas, communication etc. are made of. Form, not substance. (It strucks me that L{{\'{e}}}vi-Strauss tried to do the same with his generalized linguistics). Batesons's book, then, "is concerned with building a bridge between the facts of life and behavior and what we know today of the nature of pattern and order. \nWhy do things get in a muddle? Because of the infinitely different ways things can be only a tiny tiny bit will be perceived as "tidy". The rest is muddly. These and range of other interesting discussions with many points are available through Bateson's dialogue with his daughter. \nBateson's short essay on "experiments in thinking about observed ethnological material" elaborates on his own approach to scientific understanding, combining loose "hunches" with strict reference to established theory. In a sense it is what all of us do and the monologue appears a little forced. \nIn his discussion of national character from page 88 on, Bateson shows considerably more nuance than for instance Ruth Benedict maintaining that the concept of character is concerned with regularity rather than uniformity and takes off from the idea that people living toghether will develop similar sets of values and behaviors due to the overal holistic organization of society. His study is prior to chaos theory and since he takes so much from zoology he is obsessed with order, system, and rigidity while maintaining that science is still only models of reality. \nHe upholds that the key to understanding national character is to look for some bipolar relationship as dominant-submissive through which seemingly differentiated individuals can be said to inhabit the same traits. Relationship (interpersonal and intergroup) is definitely the key word here and he lines up another two sets of opposites (dominant-submissive; spectatorship-exhibitionist) and illustrates how the combination of all these can mark out a german national character as opposed to a Russia one. I would like to remind him first that the data he uses is quite limited and thus applying it to the whole of society is one large hindrance and that secondly, these concepts are scientific idioms and their guidance for thought can do no better than point, they can not establish facts. \nIn the chapter on Bali and the steady state, Bateson is keen to outline that he has earlier found shismogenesis to be an aspect of Iamutl society and that these can be divided into communicative and symmetrical: The term is probably borrowed from commuications engineering and refers to some sort of re- or degenerative circles of energy where an increase in A causes in increase in B which causes in increase in C etc. (the degenerative is where at some point in the chain an increase in N causes a decrease of N+1). Bateson distinguishes between a symmetrical shcismogenesis where the mutually promoting actions of A and B are essentially similar (competition, rivalry) and complementary S., where the mutually promoting actions are essentially dissimilar but mutually appropriate, as for instance in dominance-submission relations. \nBatesons makes the interesting link between passion, warfare, erotics etc. and complementary shismogenesis becaues the parties involed are using each other to get a kick and he points out that all these engagements may have much more to do with releasing schismogenetic tensions than whatever they may claim to be. It is the conscous or unconsciocus wish for release which draws the participants - a hope of reelase from tension through total involvement. Bateson concludes that "very much strengthens the case for rergarding schismogenesis and those cumulative sequences of interaction which lead to love as often psychologicaly equivalent." \nNow, this theory is general and did not apply to Bateson's Balinese data as he explains. Not because the theory was wrong but because Balinese culture (from childhood training and on) does whatever it can to contain these interactions and plead avoidance. There is a general lack of climax and many social situations ranging from the mother's play with her child unto warfare. Bali, in thi description stands opposed to schismogenetic societies of Iamutl and Radcliffe-Brown's social opposition system plus the Marxian structuralism. \nPage 117 has an interesting but short passage on spatial orientation (and social). The Balinese must not lose their sense of direction lest they become confused. \nBateson outlines different values in Balinese society and shows that contrary to both conventional economic theory and schismogenic theory, Balinese would not wish to maximize to value, prestige, self-esteem, or even submisiveness. If they wish to maximize anything (Bateson borrows from Von Neumannian game theory to explicate) it may be called stability. The urge to participate in ceremonies, do the right thing etc regardless of one's personal gain is a symptom of this ethos. Taking the idea from a game again, they all strive to keep sociey at a steady state which involves banning maximization of any odd value in favour of others. This practice, as noted, is induced in children and thus becomes a mode for interpersonal relations in contrast to for instance US. It seems much of this discussion can be phrased in terms of individualistic vs. non-individualistic societies but not wholly.},
annote = {[relink-2026-07-26: 1 file path(s) updated for 07_THEORIE folder reorg, method=basename-match, tiers=['unique']]},
author = {Bateson, Gregory},
file = {:C\:/07_THEORIE/_ToSort/Bateson_Gregory_Steps_to_an_Ecology_of_Mind.pdf:pdf},
isbn = {0345273702},
journal = {New York: Ballantine},
title = {{Steps to an ecology of mind: A revolutionary approach to man's understanding of himself}},
year = {1972}
}
