abstract = {The Architecture of Persistence argues that continued human use is the ultimate measure of sustainability in architecture, and that expanding the discourse about adaptability to include continuity as well as change oers the architectural maniestation o resilience. Why do some buildings last or generations as beloved and useful places, while others do not? How can designers today create buildings that remain useul into the uture? While architects and theorists have oered a wide range o ideas about building for change, this book focuses on persistent architecture: the material, spatial, and cultural processes that give rise to long-lived buildings. Organized in three parts, this book examines material longevity in the face of constant physical and cultural change, connects the dimensions of human use and contemporary program, and discusses how time inorms the design process. Featuring dozens o interviews with people who design and use buildings, and a close analysis of over a hundred historic and contemporary projects, the principles of persistent architecture introduced here address urgent challenges for contemporary practice while pointing towards a more sustainable built environment in the uture. The Architecture of Persistence: Designing for Future Use oers practitioners, students, and scholars a set of principles and illustrative precedents exploring architecture's unique ability to connect an instructive past, a useul present, and an unknown uture. D. Fannon is an architect and building scientist whose work integrates research and design to provide occupant comort and wellbeing in long-lasting, low-resource consuming buildings. He is jointly appointed associate professor in the School of Architecture and the Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering at Northeastern University. David earned a Bachelor o Architecture degree rom Rensselaer Polytechnic Institute, a master's rom UC Berkeley, and is a registered architect in the tate o NewYork. He is a Member o AHRAE and a LEED Accredited Proessional with B+DC specialty. M. Laboy is an assistant proessor in the chool o Architecture at Northeastern University, with ali-ate appointments in the Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering and the School of Public Policy and Urban Aairs. As co-ounder o FieLDworkshop LLC, she leads research-based transdis-ciplinary approaches to heighten the connections between people, buildings, and landscapes. Her research and teaching examine how socio-ecological thinking inuences architectural theory and practice to shape human experience, performance, and adaptability to dynamically changing environments. Michelle holds a Master o Architecture and a Master o Urban Planning rom the University o Michigan and a Bachelor o cience in Civil Engineering rom the University o Puerto Rico, and is registered as a Proessional Engineer. P. Wiederspahn is an associate proessor at Northeastern University, Boston, MA, and principal o Wiederspahn Architecture, LLC. His research and pedagogy are ocused on architectural design, production, perormance, and systems. In particular, he has conducted research on: wood construction and its cultural impact at the detail, architectural, and urban scales; high-performance, rapid-assembly, structural/thermal component construction system as an alternative to wood raming; lightweight at-pack, rapid-deployment, long-term-use emergency shelter systems; and urniture design. His architectural practice has received numerous design excellence awards or residential , multi-amily, commercial, and interior architecture projects. Peter earned his Bachelor o Architecture rom yracuse University and his Master o Architecture rom the Harvard University.},
